                "As commercial enterprises expand from middle-market businesses into large industrial conglomerates with annual turnovers spanning hundreds of millions of dollars, their working capital and capital expenditure credit requirements exceed the single-obligor exposure capacity of any individual commercial bank. Under central bank regulatory frameworks—such as the Basel Committee's Large Exposure Framework and the Reserve Bank of India's Prudential Exposure Limits—a commercial bank is strictly prohibited from lending more than 20% to 25% of its eligible Tier-1 capital base to a single corporate entity or connected business group. Consequently, large corporations must source credit from multiple financial institutions, giving rise to multi-lender banking structures: Sole Banking, Multiple Banking Arrangements (MBA), and Consortium Lending.",
                "Sole Banking vs Multiple Banking Arrangements (MBA): In Sole Banking, the corporate borrower maintains an exclusive credit relationship with a single commercial bank, which sanctions all fund-based lines, non-fund limits, and term loans. While operationally simple, sole banking exposes the bank to concentrated default risk and limits the borrower's borrowing ceiling. When borrowing needs outstrip a single bank's appetite, corporations frequently enter Multiple Banking Arrangements (MBA). In an MBA, the borrower approaches multiple independent commercial banks separately: Bank A sanctions a $30M cash credit line, Bank B sanctions a $25M letter of credit facility, and Bank C provides a $20M term loan. Crucially, in a pure MBA, each bank operates as a detached island: there is no formal agreement binding the lenders together, no unified loan documentation, and no shared monitoring protocol.",
                "The Perils and Information Asymmetry of Multiple Banking: Banking history demonstrates that uncoordinated Multiple Banking Arrangements represent a breeding ground for systemic corporate credit fraud and unmonitored over-leverage. Because banks in an MBA do not communicate continuously, unscrupulous corporate borrowers exploit information asymmetry: double-pledging the same warehouse inventory to multiple lenders, opening unmonitored current accounts at non-lending banks to siphon cash revenues, and playing lenders against each other. When distress strikes, MBA lenders engage in destructive races to seize collateral, triggering disorderly bankruptcies.",
